@node Configurations
@chapter Declaring Homes and Systems
@cindex configurations
@vindex configurations
@vindex configurations.home
@vindex configurations.system

  The @code{configurations} module describes standalone Home Manager
configurations and NixOS systems in one option tree.  A @dfn{standalone
home} has its own package set and Home Manager evaluation.  A system can
also include Home Manager settings for its users.

  Declare standalone homes under @code{configurations.home} and systems
under @code{configurations.system}.  Both default to empty submodules.
Every entry except @code{global} produces a named flake output. The
reserved @code{global} entry supplies settings shared by that kind of
configuration; it does not produce an output of its own.

@menu
* Standalone Homes::    Configure a home with its own package set.
* NixOS Systems::       Declare a system and its main module.
* Shared Settings::     Combine modules, packages, and arguments.
* System Users::        Include Home Manager in a NixOS system.
@end menu

@node Standalone Homes
@section Configuring a Standalone Home
@cindex standalone homes
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.home
@vindex homeConfigurations.@var{name}

  A standalone home entry requires a @code{home} module.  The entry name
selects the output name, not the operating-system user name.  Set
@code{home.username} and @code{home.homeDirectory} in the Home Manager
module itself.  For example:

@example
@{
  configurations = @{
    home = @{
      alice = @{
        nixpkgs = @{
          system = "x86_64-linux";
        @};

        home = @{ ... @}: @{
          home = @{
            homeDirectory = "/home/alice";

            stateVersion = "25.05";

            username = "alice";
          @};

          programs = @{
            home-manager = @{
              enable = true;
            @};
          @};
        @};
      @};
    @};
  @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
This declaration produces @code{homeConfigurations.alice}.  The
@code{stateVersion} is an example Home Manager compatibility setting;
choose it according to the history of the home you are configuring. You
can replace the inline @code{home} module with a path to a module.

  Each entry under @code{configurations.home.@var{name}} has the
following fields.  A module can be a path, an attribute set, or a module
function. A @dfn{profile} is another explicitly supplied module; infix
does not assign it a special evaluation mechanism.

@table @code
@item home
Required main Home Manager module.  There is no default.

@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.modules
@item modules
List of additional Home Manager modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.

@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.profiles
@item profiles
List of Home Manager profile modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.

@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.nixpkgs
@item nixpkgs
Package-set settings; defaults to an empty submodule.  Input and
platform selection, configuration, and overlays are described in
@ref{Shared Settings}.

@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.specialArgs
@item specialArgs
Attribute set of arguments passed to Home Manager as
@code{extraSpecialArgs}; defaults to the flake @code{inputs}. See
@ref{Shared Settings}, for the merge with global arguments.
@end table

  Home Manager receives the global home @code{modules}, the entry's
@code{modules}, its @code{profiles}, and finally its @code{home} module,
in that order.  This is the composition order; option conflicts still
follow the module system's merging and priority rules.

@node NixOS Systems
@section Configuring a NixOS System
@cindex NixOS systems
@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}
@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.system
@vindex nixosConfigurations.@var{name}

  A system entry requires a main NixOS module in @code{system}.  Its
name selects the @code{nixosConfigurations} output and supplies an
overridable default for @code{networking.hostName}.  For example, this
module declares @code{nixosConfigurations.workstation}:

@example
@{
  configurations = @{
    system = @{
      workstation = @{
        system = ./hosts/workstation/system.nix;
      @};
    @};
  @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
The example assumes that @file{./hosts/workstation/system.nix} contains
your NixOS configuration, including its hardware settings and
@code{nixpkgs.hostPlatform}.  Infix does not supply boot, filesystem, or
hardware settings.  Set @code{networking.hostName} there if the host
name should differ from @samp{workstation}.

  Each entry under @code{configurations.system.@var{name}} accepts these
fields:

@table @code
@item system
Required main NixOS module.  There is no default.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.modules
@item modules
List of additional NixOS modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.profiles
@item profiles
List of NixOS profile modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.nixpkgs
@item nixpkgs
Package-set settings; defaults to an empty submodule. @xref{Shared
Settings}.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.specialArgs
@item specialArgs
Attribute set passed to NixOS as @code{specialArgs} and to its embedded
Home Manager evaluation as @code{extraSpecialArgs}.  It defaults to the
flake @code{inputs}.  Global arguments are combined as described in
@ref{Shared Settings}.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.users
@item users
Attribute set of user declarations; defaults to @code{@{ @}}.
@xref{System Users}.
@end table

  The NixOS module list starts with the default host name, the upstream
Home Manager NixOS module, the Home Manager integration settings, and
the merged Nixpkgs settings.  It continues with global system
@code{modules}, local @code{modules}, the main @code{system} module,
local @code{profiles}, and the generated user modules.  Unlike
standalone homes, systems place the main module before their profiles.

  Embedded Home Manager uses @code{useGlobalPkgs = true} and
@code{useUserPackages = true}.  Its users share the NixOS package set,
and Home Manager installs their packages through NixOS user packages.
The module uses the standard @code{nixosConfigurations} and
@code{homeConfigurations} outputs; its intermediate
@code{finalNixOSConfigurations} and @code{finalHomeConfigurations}
options are internal.

@node Shared Settings
@section Sharing Modules and Package Settings
@cindex global settings
@cindex overlays, configuration package sets
@cindex special arguments
@vindex configurations.home.global
@vindex configurations.system.global

  The @code{global} entries let related homes or systems share modules,
Nixpkgs settings, and extra arguments.  Home globals apply to standalone
homes, and system globals apply to NixOS systems.  They are separate
submodules, each defaulting to @code{@{ @}}.

  For example, declare a common home module and an additional profile
for one home.  The paths here stand for modules supplied by your
project:

@example
@{
  configurations = @{
    home = @{
      global = @{
        modules = [
          ./home/common.nix
        ];

        nixpkgs = @{
          config = @{
            allowUnfree = true;
          @};

          system = "x86_64-linux";
        @};
      @};

      alice = @{
        home = ./home/alice.nix;

        profiles = [
          ./home/work.nix
        ];
      @};
    @};
  @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @samp{alice} home inherits the global platform and package policy.
Other homes can supply their own @code{nixpkgs} settings.

@vindex configurations.home.global.modules
@vindex configurations.system.global.modules
@vindex configurations.home.global.nixpkgs
@vindex configurations.system.global.nixpkgs
@vindex configurations.home.global.specialArgs
@vindex configurations.system.global.specialArgs
  Each global entry has @code{modules}, a list defaulting to @code{[ ]};
@code{nixpkgs}, a submodule defaulting to @code{@{ @}}; and
@code{specialArgs}, an attribute set defaulting to the flake
@code{inputs}. Global entries have no @code{profiles}, main @code{home}
or @code{system} module, or @code{users} field.

  The @code{nixpkgs} submodule has these settings, both globally and in
individual entries:

@table @code
@vindex configurations.home.global.nixpkgs.input
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.nixpkgs.input
@vindex configurations.system.global.nixpkgs.input
@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.nixpkgs.input
@item input
Nixpkgs source, accepted as a raw value.  Globally it defaults to
@code{inputs.nixpkgs}; an individual entry defaults to the corresponding
global input.  Standalone homes import this source directly.  Systems
use its @code{lib.nixosSystem} function.

@vindex configurations.home.global.nixpkgs.system
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.nixpkgs.system
@item system
Platform string for standalone homes.  The global default is
@code{"x86_64-linux"}; an individual home defaults to the global value.
This field is not a system-configuration option.  Set
@code{nixpkgs.hostPlatform} in the NixOS module instead.

@vindex configurations.home.global.nixpkgs.config
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.nixpkgs.config
@vindex configurations.system.global.nixpkgs.config
@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.nixpkgs.config
@item config
Attribute set of Nixpkgs configuration values, each accepted as a raw
value; defaults to @code{@{ @}} at both levels.  Infix combines global
and local sets with @code{//}.  A local key replaces the same global key
as a whole; this operation does not recursively merge nested sets.

@vindex configurations.home.global.nixpkgs.overlays
@vindex configurations.home.@var{name}.nixpkgs.overlays
@vindex configurations.system.global.nixpkgs.overlays
@vindex configurations.system.@var{name}.nixpkgs.overlays
@item overlays
List of raw overlay values; defaults to @code{[ ]} at both levels. Local
overlays follow the global overlays in the combined list.
@end table

  Special arguments use the same right-biased operation:
@code{global.specialArgs // local.specialArgs}.  This supplies
@code{extraSpecialArgs} for a standalone home and @code{specialArgs} for
a system.  Each local set defaults to @code{inputs}, so a same-named
input in that set can replace a global argument.  Declare the desired
local arguments explicitly when those names overlap.

@node System Users
@section Including Home Manager Users in a System
@cindex system users
@cindex reusing home configurations

  A declaration at
@code{configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}} combines a
NixOS user module with Home Manager settings.  Both @code{user} and
@code{home} are required.  The entry name selects
@code{home-manager.users.@var{user}}; your NixOS module must declare the
matching operating-system user.

@menu
* Direct Home Modules:: Supply Home Manager settings for one user.
* Reusing Homes::       Share a standalone home's module list.
* User Options::        Reference for each user declaration.
@end menu

@node Direct Home Modules
@subsection Supplying a User's Home Module

  To include a Home Manager module directly, assign it to the user's
@code{home} field.  For example, add this declaration to a system that
already has a main @code{system} module:

@example
@{
  configurations = @{
    system = @{
      workstation = @{
        users = @{
          alice = @{
            home = ./home/alice.nix;

            modules = [
              ./home/common.nix
            ];

            profiles = [
              ./home/work.nix
            ];

            user = @{ ... @}: @{
              users = @{
                users = @{
                  alice = @{
                    extraGroups = [
                      "wheel"
                    ];

                    isNormalUser = true;
                  @};
                @};
              @};
            @};
          @};
        @};
      @};
    @};
  @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
The Home Manager user imports this entry's @code{modules}, then its
@code{profiles}, and finally @code{home}.  Global standalone-home
modules are not included by this form.

@node Reusing Homes
@subsection Reusing a Standalone Home's Modules

  To reuse an existing standalone home, set the system user's
@code{home} to its evaluated declaration through the flake module's
@code{config} argument.  The following example assumes that the home
@samp{alice}, the system @samp{workstation}, and its NixOS user module
are declared elsewhere in the same configuration:

@example
@{
  config,
  ...
@}:
@{
  configurations = @{
    system = @{
      workstation = @{
        users = @{
          alice = @{
            home = config.configurations.home.alice;
          @};
        @};
      @};
    @};
  @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
This form imports the global home modules, the referenced home's
@code{modules}, its @code{profiles}, and its main @code{home} module.
The system user entry's own @code{modules} and @code{profiles} are
ignored. Pass the configuration value itself, rather than an output from
@code{homeConfigurations}.

  Reuse transfers only the Home Manager module list.  It does not
transfer the standalone home's @code{nixpkgs} or @code{specialArgs}.
The embedded home uses the NixOS package set and the system's merged
special arguments, as described in @ref{NixOS Systems} and @ref{Shared
Settings}.

@node User Options
@subsection User Declaration Options

  The fields below belong to
@code{configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}}.  They
configure a single embedded Home Manager user, not a standalone home.

@table @code
@vindex configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}.user
@item user
Required NixOS module declaring the operating-system user.  It can be a
module path, attribute set, or function.  There is no default.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}.home
@item home
Required raw value: either a Home Manager module or an evaluated
@code{configurations.home.@var{name}} entry.  There is no default.
@xref{Direct Home Modules}, and @ref{Reusing Homes}, for the two forms.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}.modules
@item modules
List of Home Manager modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.  Used only with a
direct home module.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}.profiles
@item profiles
List of Home Manager profile modules; defaults to @code{[ ]}.  Used only
with a direct home module.

@vindex configurations.system.@var{host}.users.@var{user}.specialArgs
@item specialArgs
Attribute set defaulting to @code{inputs}.  This field is declared but
is not passed to Home Manager by the current implementation.  Put
arguments needed by embedded users in the system's @code{specialArgs}
instead.
@end table
